## Result

The format, verbatim — **absolute counts, failures grouped by class, and a stated next action.** Far more persuasive than any accuracy percentage.

```
NN runs · NN passed · NN failed
  N × <failure class>
  N × <failure class>
→ <are these classes addressable? what happens next?>
```

**Failures are not something to minimise.** They are the input to the next iteration, and showing them is what makes the passes credible. A report with no failures reads as a report that was not run honestly.

<!-- table:eval-report.four-tests role=labels key=Test -->

## The four tests

| Test | Question | Passed | Failed | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 01 | Did it have the right data? | | | |
| 02 | Did it take the required steps, including the compliance ones? | | | |
| 03 | Does it match an expert? | | | |
| 04 | Is it safe to act on? | | | |

Test 04: a system that correctly declines to act is **passing**. Score the routing decision, not the absence of an action.

